WEBVTT

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:07.410
When I began this project, I was
choosing between the history of money

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:09.990
and the development of architecture.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:15.710
I chose money because it connects
ancient objects with everyday life.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.970
Jiaozi first caught my attention.

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:23.570
From there, I wanted to understand what
came before paper money, what followed,

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:26.270
and why older forms continued to matter.

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:34.810
I chose a website because I
wanted to bring photographs,

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:39.090
historical writing,
and interaction together.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:45.390
My early versions included merchants,
game-like choices, and long explanations.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:50.190
As I added more, the website
became harder to follow.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:55.930
Based on the consultation feedback,
I reconsidered the audience first.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:02.600
I set the audience as students
who frequently use cash or

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:08.910
electronic payments, but are
new to Chinese currency history.

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:13.310
I then clarified the message and the form.

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:17.370
That led to three modes
with different purposes.

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:23.410
The museum for close looking,
flashcards for focused questions,

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:26.810
and the history view
for fuller explanation.

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:33.770
My first reflection concerns
political authority.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:37.860
The course's discussion
of Qin standardisation

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:43.510
and the Han Salt and Iron Debate helped me
see money as part of governing an empire.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:50.900
The Records of the Grand Historian
describes a Han policy

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:55.650
that restricted minting
to three imperial offices.

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:59.850
I interpret this as a change
in who controlled money.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:05.150
Shared standards could make coins
more familiar across regions,

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:08.320
while centralised minting
strengthened government control.

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:12.840
I now understand that a reform
could improve coordination

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:16.170
and concentrate power at the same time.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:23.050
My second reflection is
about what people trust.

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:27.570
Paper was easier to carry
than heavy iron coins.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:33.910
But a jiaozi holder also needed confidence
that others would accept the note,

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:38.780
and that the issuer could
exchange it for coins as agreed.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:44.800
The History of the Song describes
private issuers whose finances

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:49.850
weakened until they could no longer
exchange holders' notes for coins.

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:57.690
This showed me that trust depended on
practical ability as well as expectations.

00:02:58.570 --> 00:02:59.810
For trust to last,

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:05.470
the issuer needed the resources
to fulfil its promise.

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:10.710
This question also matters today.

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:14.220
Research on WeChat Pay and Alipay
found that greater trust was

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:18.210
associated with a stronger intention
to continue using the platform.

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:23.470
For me, this connects monetary
history with human expectations.

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:28.880
When people choose a payment method, they
need confidence that it will be accepted

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:31.490
and that the payment can be completed.

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:34.110
Convenience and trust work together.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:39.040
Making the website also changed
how I communicate history.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:44.760
I learned to explain my interpretations
clearly, support them with evidence,

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:47.820
and recognise that some
questions remain open.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:51.080
This is what applied
history means in my project:

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:54.450
using the past to understand
familiar experiences today

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.860
and using a digital format to help
others make that connection.

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:00.760
Thank you.
